<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demak Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sultanate of Demak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Demak Sultanate was the first major Muslim state on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, based on the island's north coast. Its rise in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries contributed to the final collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> and to the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> across Java.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Demak Sultanate" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mhatopzz · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUS_Koin_Sultan_Pangeran_Raden_Fatah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Demak lay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s north coast, at the site of the present-day city. It began as a port <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Hindu-Buddhist Majapahit kingdom, and Muslim traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudera-pasai-sultanate">Samudra Pasai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to the northern ports. Tradition attributes the foundation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-patah">Raden Patah</a>, a Javanese noble related to Majapahit royalty, who set up an Islamic learning centre at Glagah Wangi, a base of the missionaries known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>. After the fall of Kertabhumi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trowulan">Trowulan</a> in 1478, Demak stopped sending tribute to the Majapahit court and declared its independence. The kingdom was established, by a chronogram, in 1481, and Raden Patah built a Grand Mosque and made members of the Wali Songo his advisers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Demak gained hegemony over the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresik">Gresik</a>, and overlordship of Jambi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. It defeated Daha, the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, in 1518. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pati-unus">Pati Unus</a> led fleets of Javanese ports against the Portuguese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, with about 100 ships in 1513 and 375 in 1521. Both failed, and the losses hurt the ports badly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Trenggana</a>, crowned in 1522, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatahillah">Fatahillah</a> in 1527 to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kelapa</a> from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>, creating the vassal Sultanate of Banten, and conquered the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> remnants of Majapahit. He was killed in 1546 campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasuruan">Pasuruan</a>.</p><p>His death set off a civil war. Sunan Prawata was killed in 1549 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-penangsang">Arya Penangsang</a> of Jipang, who took the throne but was overthrown by a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a> of Pajang. In 1568 Hadiwijaya moved Demak's regalia to Pajang and made Demak its vassal, ending the sultanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Demak lived by trade, importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> wrote that it had more inhabitants than any other port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. As the first Islamic polity in Java it has a venerated status among Indonesian Muslims, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-demak">Great Mosque of Demak</a> is widely believed to be the oldest surviving mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demak Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rebellious vassals and ambitious coastal rulers, above all the <b>Sultanate of Demak</b>, further undermined cohesion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
